  - scoping
body: |-
  Context: continues from the storefront+auth build. Storefront (React/Vite SPA) + email/password JWT auth are built, committed (04459e3), and now runtime-validated locally (Neon connected, products seeded, signup/login working, grid rendering). Fixed a CORS bug along the way: server/src/index.ts used `cors({ origin: process.env.CLIENT_ORIGIN ?? true })` — `??` treats an empty CLIENT_ORIGIN= from .env as a real value, so cors matched origin "" and sent no Access-Control-Allow-Origin; changed to `|| true`.

  Existing buy flow ($checkout-flow) is coded end-to-end: client Buy button -> POST /checkout (JWT) -> spend policy (per-tx $50 / daily $200 caps, $10 auto-approve threshold w/ 428 confirm) -> per-user Circle developer-controlled wallet (ARC-TESTNET) -> USDC transfer -> order 'submitted' -> Circle webhook flips to confirmed/failed. To actually execute a purchase it needs: MERCHANT_PAYOUT_ADDRESS (currently empty), valid Circle creds + registered entity secret + funded wallet, and (for confirmation) a publicly reachable webhook. Known code gap: /checkout has no try/catch or async error middleware, so an async throw (e.g. missing payout address) hangs the request in Express 4. User explicitly said the real Circle purchase is NOT the priority right now.

  The priority is the agentic layer: a "merchant MCP" (name TBD) running on the user's machine so their AI agent can browse this store and initiate purchases on their behalf, gated by the user's World ID selfie check.

  Next session: ideate the "hard part" — the agent purchase-initiation flow and the selfie handoff (Options A/B/C above), plus the agent-grant auth mechanism that makes agent-initiation unspoofable.
confidence: 0.8
